Z||000018||GodSend||08-14-2001||05:27 PM||||I had a brain storm, untill I looked and realized there are two hinges on each door .. here is my idea anyways  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Basically if you look at the way the hinge is, it would be very easy to limit the travel of the door so that It only open about 1 foot (20 degrees) from the body .  Having said that.  I invisioned a hinge which would bolt between the current hinge and the body, which would be hindge in the upper left corner (if we are talking about the drivers side). .. The hinge itself would look something like a convential one you would find on a door.<P>  The idea is that the door opens 20 normally (to clear any issues you have with the strikers, and properly closing).. and then pivots up, not like the above picture. more like...<BR>     Picture standing in front of the car... and being able to see a 3/4 view of the door of both doors at the same time (obviously if they swing totally out, you can never park close to anyone) ... <P>Does anyone understand what I am getting at?<BR>The main problem would be ordering the hinges (and providing a system to keep the door up).  By this I mean that.. when opening, the normal hinge words first, then pivot up... and during closing, its opposite..).,.||||reg||1
Z||000019||grinthock||08-14-2001||06:41 PM||||GodSend, without looking at tolerances, I think you are right, that's the right IDEA, to hold it up I think Rodney could help us with one of his coolio struts, he seems to be the strut master!  Something like a vertical gas strut on a pivot mount to hold it up... If you wanted to go motorized that would be MESSEY but it would have to almost cable driven, AKA, the cable is connected to the door, and via a pulley pulls on it in such a way to pull the right force on the pivot points.||||reg||1
Z||000020||rogergarrison||08-14-2001||07:11 PM||||As was said in last thread on this, dont think anyone is going to make it work without spending thousands of dollars. The body shape/side dont lend itself to the conversion. Even if you finally did figure it out door is way too heavy stock, youd almost have to make a thin glass or fiber door to be able to lift it without a very very complicated assortment of lifts. Ill be the first to pat the back of anyone who does it. Ive done some pretty radical custom body work and I really dont see it happening.||||reg||1
